= Welcome to Ezetta <sec-welcome>

This is an example project: it explains Ezetta and shows at the same time what can be written with it. Everything you read here is a text in Ezetta: the headings, the footnotes, the citations, the figures and these paragraphs themselves. You can click any place and see how it was made.

You are working on a copy. Write, delete and try out whatever you like; the original that comes with the program stays unchanged. If you want to start over, open the example project on the start screen again and choose "Start over".

Read the sections in order or jump around: each one explains one thing and shows it on itself. The outline (Cmd+3 on a Mac, Ctrl+3 elsewhere) shows all headings at a glance, and a click on one jumps to it.

== What Ezetta is <sec-what>

Ezetta is a writing program for long texts with citations. You write in sections, which you arrange in groups, and while you write you already see what citations, footnotes and figures will look like. How the finished pages look is decided only by the export: the same texts can be set in a completely different way with another template, without you touching them.

The texts are ordinary files. Each section is a text file in the format #link("https://typst.app")[Typst], in a small, fixed selection. Nothing lies in a hidden format, and the files stay readable even without Ezetta.
